From: BeeWi BC7PW bring-up <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] mt7601sta: print pAd at each rate-switch step of MlmePeriodicExec()

Temporary diagnostic, revised. The previous revision bracketed the two
rate-switching calls by text alone, and one of the two markers never appeared in
any log -- impossible for an adjacent pair -- so the ordering could not be
trusted. These four print the adapter pointer itself, which answers the question
that matters: MlmeDynamicTxRateSwitching() is called with a corrupted pAd
(r0 = 0x07060504, MCS-index bytes) and dies on its first dereference, so we need
to know *when* the caller's pAd goes bad.

  PEXEC 1/2-before/after-apdrs   around APMlmeDynamicTxRateSwitching()
  PEXEC 3/4-before/after-drs     around MlmeDynamicTxRateSwitching()

Read it as: pAd bad at 3-before-drs means the AP-side call just corrupted it;
valid at 3 but bad at 1 means an earlier pass did; bad at 1 already means the
previous tick left the damage. The old entry marker is dropped -- it needed a
brace-relative anchor, and a previous generator slip put a statement into the
parameter list that way.
--- a/common/mlme.c
+++ b/common/mlme.c
@@ -976,7 +976,9 @@
 	{
 #ifdef CONFIG_AP_SUPPORT
 		IF_DEV_CONFIG_OPMODE_ON_AP(pAd)
+	printk(KERN_ERR "PEXEC 1-before-apdrs pAd=%p\n", pAd);
 			APMlmeDynamicTxRateSwitching(pAd);
+	printk(KERN_ERR "PEXEC 2-after-apdrs pAd=%p\n", pAd);
 #endif /* CONFIG_AP_SUPPORT */
 #ifdef CONFIG_STA_SUPPORT
 		/* perform dynamic tx rate switching based on past TX history*/
@@ -991,7 +993,9 @@
 #endif
 					)
 				&& (!OPSTATUS_TEST_FLAG(pAd, fOP_STATUS_DOZE)))
+	printk(KERN_ERR "PEXEC 3-before-drs pAd=%p\n", pAd);
 				MlmeDynamicTxRateSwitching(pAd);
+	printk(KERN_ERR "PEXEC 4-after-drs pAd=%p\n", pAd);
 		}
 #endif /* CONFIG_STA_SUPPORT */
 	}
